digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Domain consensus: Multiple successful applications of a technique across different biomolecule types indicate the technique's broad applicability"];
  "N12" [label="(6,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, ExM enables visualization of anatomical features through dense labeling of total protein via NHS ester staining"];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If dense labeling of proteins reveals anatomical features, then dense labeling of membranes could reveal membrane ultrastructure"];
  "N14" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Currently ExM uses dense protein labeling for anatomy, and if dense protein labeling reveals features then dense membrane labeling could reveal ultrastructure, therefore dense membrane labeling is a promising approach Abduction-knowledge: Dense labeling strategy can reveal biological structures"];
  "N15" [label="(7,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Achieving dense labeling in fixed tissues has remained challenging despite several ExM methods for membrane/lipid labeling"];
  "N17" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Domain consensus: If a widely needed capability remains challenging despite multiple attempts, then a novel technical solution is required"];
  "N19" [label="(8,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, ideal membrane labeling should be uniform, continuous, high signal-to-background ratio, preserve ultrastructure, and enable protein visualization in fixed tissues"];
  "N2" [label="(1,1,3) Deduction-rule: If a swellable polymer network is synthesized within a specimen, then the specimen can be physically expanded"];
  "N20" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If membrane labeling meets these ideal criteria, then it would enable comprehensive ultrastructural analysis"];
  "N22" [label="(9,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, such membrane labeling would enable nanoscale protein imaging relative to membrane landmarks and facilitate segmentation/tracing of membranous structures like axons and dendrites"];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If membrane structures can be segmented and traced, then neuronal circuit analysis becomes feasible"];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Technical gap exists in dense membrane labeling"];
  "N30" [label="(11,0,0) Induction-case: We rationally designed innovative membrane labeling probes and optimized ExM protocol to achieve dense membrane labeling"];
  "N31" [label="(12,0,0) Induction-case: Our probe labels plasma, mitochondrial, nuclear, ciliary membranes, myelin sheaths, and extracellular vesicle membranes in fixed mouse brain"];
  "N32" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Domain consensus: If a probe successfully labels multiple membrane types across diverse cellular compartments, then the probe has general membrane labeling capability"];
  "N34" [label="(14,0,0) Induction-case: umExM preserves ultrastructure, visualizes membranes in 100µm brain slices at ~60nm resolution with excellent uniformity, continuity, and 40-80 fold signal-to-background ratio"];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Domain consensus: If achieved characteristics match ideal requirements, then the solution successfully addresses the problem"];
  "N4" [label="(2,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, when hydrogel swells, anchored biomolecules are pulled apart isotropically to ~4-10x magnification"];
  "N40" [label="(15,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, umExM supports co-visualization of membranes with proteins and RNAs"];
  "N42" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If multimodal visualization and neuronal tracing are enabled, then comprehensive tissue analysis is possible"];
  "N44" [label="(17,0,0) Induction-case: umExM combined with optical fluctuation imaging and iterative expansion achieves ~35nm resolution with standard confocal microscope"];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Domain consensus: If resolution can be further improved through method combinations, then the technique has extensibility"];
  "N8" [label="(5,0,0) Induction-case: ExM protocols are used for visualizing proteins, nucleic acids, and membranes/lipids"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate ultrastructural membrane expansion microscopy, combining rational membrane-anchoring probes with optimized expansion microscopy chemistry, to achieve dense, uniform, high-signal membrane labeling in fixed tissue for ultrastructural visualization, segmentation, tracing, multimodal analysis, and higher-resolution imaging on standard confocal microscopes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When an imaging field has a clear technical gap, defined desirable requirements, a plausible probe-and-chemistry strategy, and evidence that the resulting method labels membranes densely while enabling analysis and extensibility, these conclusions jointly support developing and evaluating that method as a research aim."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Given the technical gap in dense membrane labeling, rationally designed membrane-labeling probes together with optimized ExM chemistry are a plausible solution approach for achieving dense membrane labeling."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) In ExM, anchoring biomolecules to the polymer network and swelling the hydrogel physically separates nearby biomolecules to enlarged, more resolvable distances."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because umExM supports co-visualization of membranes with proteins and RNAs and enables segmentation and tracing of neuronal processes, it supports comprehensive tissue analysis."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The continuing challenge of dense membrane labeling in fixed tissues indicates a technical gap that motivates a novel technical solution."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) The listed ideal membrane-labeling criteria are desirable technical requirements because a method meeting them would enable comprehensive ultrastructural analysis."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Membrane labeling that enables segmentation and tracing of axons and dendrites would make neuronal circuit analysis more feasible."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because ExM is used to visualize multiple biomolecule classes and enables nanoscale imaging in preserved specimens, it has demonstrated broad applicability for biological imaging contexts involving those targets."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The probe demonstrates general membrane-labeling capability across multiple membrane types and cellular compartments in fixed mouse brain."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) umExM meets the stated ideal technical requirements by providing uniform, continuous, high-signal membrane labeling while preserving ultrastructure."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) umExM is extensible toward higher-resolution imaging through method combinations such as optical fluctuation imaging and iterative expansion."];
